WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.785
Your agent, in your terminal.

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:10.756
One command opens a full screen chat that streams, calls tools, and remembers.

00:00:12.056 --> 00:00:14.317
Answers stream in as formatted Markdown.

00:00:14.637 --> 00:00:19.032
Tool calls show their arguments and timing, and code is coloured by language.

00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:26.303
Type slash for the commands: more than thirty, for sessions, models, notes and skills.

00:00:26.623 --> 00:00:27.860
Tab completes them.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.080
Press question mark for every key.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.728
Escape stops the agent, and typing while it works steers it.

00:00:38.028 --> 00:00:43.575
Name a conversation, fork it to try another approach, and pick up any earlier one with resume.

00:00:45.875 --> 00:00:50.248
Mention a file with at, run a command with bang, or call a function with hash.

00:00:50.568 --> 00:00:52.638
The output goes with your next message.

00:00:54.937 --> 00:00:56.793
Switch models mid conversation.

00:00:57.113 --> 00:01:01.764
Ping checks that one answers, and context shows how much of the window you have used.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:09.248
Make the prompt yours: five styles, and widgets for the branch, the clock and the context.

00:01:11.548 --> 00:01:12.529
It remembers.

00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:19.207
Notes about you and its work go into every conversation, and a vault keeps secrets sealed, never shown again.

00:01:21.507 --> 00:01:28.184
See its skills and sub-agents, connect services like GitHub or Notion, and check the tokens it has used.

00:01:30.484 --> 00:01:36.820
Export a conversation as Markdown or a web page, copy the last answer, or record the screen to share.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:46.587
And a wizard that asks what your agent should do, from the model and tools to channels and the look of the chat, then writes the project.

00:01:48.087 --> 00:01:49.239
BxAgents.

00:01:49.559 --> 00:01:52.204
Build your agent, then talk to it in your terminal.

